# Capacity Aware Dispatcher Design

## I wanted to change the behavior, not replace the experiment

Once I had a working baseline, I started thinking about what the elevators were actually doing.

The baseline dispatcher was intentionally simple: one request gets assigned to an available elevator, the elevator handles it, and then becomes available again.

That works as a starting point.

But there's something hard to ignore.

An elevator has a capacity of six people, yet the baseline was effectively treating every trip as if it were meant for one person.

If an elevator picked up one passenger going from floor 2 to floor 8, the other five spaces were still empty.

A passenger waiting on floor 5 might even be heading in roughly the same direction, but the elevator had no way to consider serving them along the way.

Instead of thinking about each request as an isolated trip, could an elevator pick up additional passengers along its route, as long as there was still room?

This became my first attempt at making the simulation behave more like a real multi passenger elevator system.

---

## Keeping the baseline

I didn't want to modify the original dispatcher until it became something completely different.

The baseline was useful.

It gave me a simple reference point that I could eventually compare against.

So I treated it as a control rather than something that needed to be replaced.

The new dispatcher would have to answer a different question:

> If the elevator already has somewhere to go, can we use that route to serve additional requests?

That meant I needed to preserve the original system while adding another strategy around it.

This distinction mattered because I wasn't just trying to make the elevator better.

I wanted to create another experiment.

If the capacity aware version eventually performs differently, I want to be able to ask why.

---

## The first architectural problem

At first, the problem sounded like a dispatcher problem.

The dispatcher decides which elevator receives a request, so I naturally started there.

But the more I looked at the existing system, the more obvious it became that the dispatcher alone couldn't accomplish what I wanted.

The simulation engine had an assumption built into it:

one elevator → one active request → one destination.

Even if the dispatcher decided that an elevator should serve another passenger along the way, the rest of the simulation wasn't structured to represent that behavior.

This was an important discovery.

The problem wasn't simply:

> "How do I write a smarter dispatcher?"

It was:

> "What does the rest of the system need to understand before a smarter dispatcher can actually exist?"

That changed the design.

---

## Thinking in routes instead of targets

The baseline could largely think in terms of a target:

Elevator → Floor 8

The capacity aware version needed something more like:

Elevator → Floor 5 → Floor 8 → Floor 10

The elevator needed a sequence of stops.

I introduced a route made up of explicit Stop objects so that an elevator could have several destinations associated with its current trip.

This also made the idea of a directional sweep useful.

Instead of constantly reconsidering every elevator from scratch, an elevator could continue in its current direction and potentially pick up passengers whose requests were ahead of it.

This is similar in spirit to SCAN/LOOK style movement: continue serving useful stops in one direction rather than immediately reversing whenever another request appears.

I wasn't trying to reproduce a production elevator controller.

I was looking for a simple routing model that captured the behavior I wanted to experiment with.

---

## When can a new passenger join the route?

Once elevators could have routes, the next question was deciding when a waiting request could be merged into one.

I wanted the rules to be deliberately conservative.

A waiting passenger could be considered for an elevator when:

* the elevator was already moving in a compatible direction
* the passenger's pickup floor was ahead of the elevator
* the elevator still had enough capacity
* adding the pickup would make sense within the current route

For passengers who didn't fit those conditions, the request could wait for another opportunity or be assigned elsewhere.

This wasn't intended to be the perfect elevator algorithm.

I wanted something understandable enough that I could later measure its behavior and improve it.

---

## Capacity is more than counting people inside

Another design problem appeared when I thought about capacity.

It isn't enough to ask:

> How many passengers are inside the elevator right now?

Suppose an elevator currently has four passengers inside and has already committed to picking up three more people along its route.

At the moment, only four people are physically inside.

But treating the elevator as having two remaining spaces would allow the dispatcher to promise more passengers than it can actually carry.

So the capacity check needed to consider both:

current riders + pending pickups


That made capacity a property of the planned route, not just the elevator's current physical state.

I chose a conservative approach here.

If the elevator has already effectively reserved its remaining capacity for passengers on the route, I don't want another request to assume that space is available.

It may mean that some opportunities are missed.

That's acceptable for this version.

I would rather have a simple rule I can reason about than a complicated optimization whose behavior I can't explain yet.

---

## Reusing elevators that are already moving

This was the part of the idea I was most interested in.

The baseline asks:

> Which elevator is available for this request?

The capacity aware version asks something slightly different:

> Can an elevator that is already moving also serve this request?

That changes the meaning of an available elevator.

An elevator doesn't necessarily need to be idle to be useful.

It might already be travelling through the exact floor where another passenger is waiting.

That means the dispatcher can make use of work that is already happening instead of treating every new request as a completely separate job.

This is where the simulation started becoming more interesting to me.

The goal wasn't simply to reduce the number of trips.

It was to make the system reason about shared movement.

---

## The new simulation layer

Once the dispatcher could produce multi stop routes, the simulation engine had to understand them.

This was the part I initially underestimated.

The dispatcher could say:

Elevator 1:
    pick up A
    pick up B
    drop off A
    pick up C
    drop off B
    drop off C

But the simulation needed to know when each of those things actually happened.

A request being added to a route does not mean a passenger has boarded.

A passenger might still be waiting on the floor.

That meant the simulation had to distinguish between:

* a planned stop
* a pickup
* a passenger actually boarding
* a passenger being carried
* a drop off
* a completed request

This is why the capacity aware version couldn't simply be another few lines inside the dispatcher.

The behavior required a different execution model.

---

## Keeping the experiment contained

I also wanted to make this change without rewriting the existing implementation.

So I treated the capacity aware system as an extension around the baseline architecture rather than editing the original code everywhere.

That constraint was useful.

It forced me to ask:

> What does the new behavior actually require?

rather than:

> How can I modify the old code until it happens to work?

The result was a separate capacity aware simulation and dispatcher that could operate alongside the original system.

That gave me two things I wanted:

1. the baseline remained intact
2. the new behavior could be developed independently

It also made the comparison much cleaner.

---

## Some limitations were intentional

The first version was not meant to solve every possible routing problem.

For example, I kept the routing fairly conservative.

An elevator would generally commit to a directional sweep rather than constantly reversing direction.

Requests that arrived too late to fit naturally into the current pass might have to wait for another pass.

There are also situations where a more sophisticated controller could make a better choice than my current rules.

That's fine.

At this stage, I'm more interested in having a clear strategy that I can test than in trying to produce the final elevator algorithm.

The whole point of the Playground is that the first version doesn't have to be the final answer.

---

## What this experiment changed for me

The interesting part of this stage wasn't just adding capacity.

It changed how I thought about the system.

I started with what looked like a local problem:

> "Make the dispatcher smarter."

But the actual problem crossed several layers.

Changing one behavior exposed assumptions everywhere else.

That is something I expect to keep happening throughout the Playground.

A system can have all the right components and still be designed around assumptions that prevent a new idea from working.

The only way to discover those assumptions is to try to push the system somewhere it wasn't originally designed to go.

---

## Where this leaves the experiment

At this point, I had two different ways of handling elevator requests.

One treated requests mostly as individual trips.

The other tried to reuse existing elevator movement and combine compatible requests into shared routes.

Now I had something worth testing.

But before I could compare the two properly, I needed to know whether either implementation was actually behaving correctly.

And that turned out to be a much bigger problem than I expected.

